<section class="ex-hero"><div class="ex-w ex-hero-g"><div><p class="ex-pill">Revision that sticks</p><h1>Walk into the exam knowing you are ready.</h1><p class="ex-lead">ExamPrep is an independent study guide for anyone facing an exam. Build a revision timetable, plan focused study sessions and track your past-paper scores with three free planners.</p><p class="ex-btns"><a class="btn" href="#planners">Build my timetable</a><a class="ex-btn2" href="revision-techniques-guide.html">How to revise</a></p></div>
<figure class="ex-hero-f"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1759984782106-4b56d0aa05b8?auto=format&fit=crop&w=720&h=640&q=75" alt="young woman wearing headphones studying at a desk" width="720" height="640" fetchpriority="high"><div class="ex-chip ex-chip1"><b>25 min</b>focus blocks</div><div class="ex-chip ex-chip2"><b>3 passes</b>of every topic</div></figure></div></section>

<section class="ex-s"><div class="ex-w"><div class="ex-stats"><div><b>6&ndash;8</b><span>weeks of revision before a major exam</span></div><div><b>20%</b><span>of your time kept for past papers</span></div><div><b>5 min</b><span>break after each focus block</span></div><div><b>1 page</b><span>summary per topic for the final week</span></div></div></div></section>

<section class="ex-s ex-tint"><div class="ex-w"><p class="ex-eyebrow">The method</p><h2>Revise in three passes</h2><div class="ex-three"><article><span>1</span><h3>Learn</h3><p>Go through each topic once with your notes open. Turn every heading into a question and write short answers in your own words.</p></article><article><span>2</span><h3>Recall</h3><p>Close the notes. Answer the same questions from memory, check, and mark the ones you missed. Repeat those a few days later.</p></article><article><span>3</span><h3>Apply</h3><p>Sit past papers under timed conditions. Mark them honestly and write down why each mark was lost.</p></article></div></div></section>

<section class="ex-s" id="planners"><div class="ex-w"><p class="ex-eyebrow">Free planners</p><h2>Three planners, no sign-up</h2><p class="ex-sub">Everything is worked out in your browser. Nothing you type is stored or sent.</p><div class="ex-tools">
<div class="ex-tool"><div class="ex-tool-h"><h3>Revision Timetable Builder</h3><p>Share your hours fairly between subjects.</p></div><div class="ex-tool-b"><label for="rt-s">Subjects<input id="rt-s" type="number" min="1" max="15" value="5" inputmode="numeric"></label><label for="rt-d">Days until the first exam<input id="rt-d" type="number" min="1" max="300" value="42" inputmode="numeric"></label><label for="rt-h">Study hours a day<input id="rt-h" type="number" min="0.5" max="12" step="0.5" value="3" inputmode="decimal"></label><label for="rt-r">Rest days a week<select id="rt-r"><option value="0">None</option><option value="1" selected>One</option><option value="2">Two</option></select></label><div class="ex-res" id="rt-o" aria-live="polite"></div></div></div>
<div class="ex-tool"><div class="ex-tool-h"><h3>Study Session Planner</h3><p>Split a study period into blocks and breaks.</p></div><div class="ex-tool-b"><label for="ss-t">Start time<input id="ss-t" type="time" value="16:00"></label><label for="ss-m">Time available (minutes)<input id="ss-m" type="number" min="20" max="600" value="150" inputmode="numeric"></label><label for="ss-f">Focus block<select id="ss-f"><option value="25" selected>25 minutes</option><option value="40">40 minutes</option><option value="50">50 minutes</option></select></label><div class="ex-res" id="ss-o" aria-live="polite"></div></div></div>
<div class="ex-tool"><div class="ex-tool-h"><h3>Past Paper Tracker</h3><p>See how far you are from your target.</p></div><div class="ex-tool-b"><label for="pp-m">Marks scored<input id="pp-m" type="number" min="0" max="1000" value="52" inputmode="numeric"></label><label for="pp-t">Total marks<input id="pp-t" type="number" min="1" max="1000" value="80" inputmode="numeric"></label><label for="pp-g">Target (%)<input id="pp-g" type="number" min="1" max="100" value="75" inputmode="numeric"></label><label for="pp-x">Exam length (minutes)<input id="pp-x" type="number" min="10" max="300" value="90" inputmode="numeric"></label><div class="ex-res" id="pp-o" aria-live="polite"></div></div></div>
</div></div></section>

<section class="ex-s ex-ind"><div class="ex-w ex-split"><div><p class="ex-eyebrow">What works</p><h2>Techniques ranked by evidence</h2><p>Researchers who compare study methods keep reaching the same conclusion: the techniques that feel easiest are usually the weakest.</p></div><div class="tblw"><table class="ex-tbl"><thead><tr><th>Technique</th><th>Effect</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Practice testing</td><td>High</td><td>Forces you to retrieve</td></tr><tr><td>Spaced revision</td><td>High</td><td>Interrupts forgetting</td></tr><tr><td>Mixing topics</td><td>Medium</td><td>Trains choosing a method</td></tr><tr><td>Explaining aloud</td><td>Medium</td><td>Exposes gaps</td></tr><tr><td>Rereading</td><td>Low</td><td>Feels familiar, fades fast</td></tr><tr><td>Highlighting</td><td>Low</td><td>No thinking required</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></section>

<section class="ex-s"><div class="ex-w ex-desk"><figure><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1769794370964-78412732f1cd?auto=format&fit=crop&w=420&h=520&q=75" alt="desk with papers, pens and a highlighter" width="420" height="520" loading="lazy"></figure><div><p class="ex-eyebrow">Set up once</p><h2>A desk that helps you start</h2><ul class="ex-ticks"><li>Only the current subject on the desk</li><li>Phone in another room, not on silent beside you</li><li>A visible timer for each block</li><li>Water, and a snack for the long break</li><li>Tomorrow&rsquo;s first task written on a sticky note before you stop</li></ul></div><figure><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1761546571631-a4d61b55cd2f?auto=format&fit=crop&w=420&h=520&q=75" alt="desk with a calculator, pens and study papers" width="420" height="520" loading="lazy"></figure></div></section>

<section class="ex-s ex-tint"><div class="ex-w"><p class="ex-eyebrow">Full guides</p><h2>Read these before you plan</h2><div class="ex-gds"><a class="ex-gd" href="revision-techniques-guide.html"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1769794371008-954a71d95ce8?auto=format&fit=crop&w=640&h=360&q=75" alt="yellow highlighter resting on handwritten notes" width="640" height="360" loading="lazy"><div><span>Guide 1 &middot; 8 min</span><h3>The Revision Techniques Guide</h3><p>Active recall, spaced repetition, blank-page summaries and how to use past papers properly.</p></div></a><a class="ex-gd" href="exam-day-guide.html"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1752578753798-ff3a23e16498?auto=format&fit=crop&w=640&h=360&q=75" alt="papers, pens and highlighters laid out on a table" width="640" height="360" loading="lazy"><div><span>Guide 2 &middot; 7 min</span><h3>The Exam Day Guide</h3><p>The night before, the first five minutes, dividing your time by marks and what to do when you get stuck.</p></div></a></div></div></section>

<section class="ex-s"><div class="ex-w ex-wk"><div><p class="ex-eyebrow">An example</p><h2>What one good study day looks like</h2><ol class="ex-day"><li><time>4:00</time><div><b>Block 1: Maths</b>Ten practice questions on one topic.</div></li><li><time>4:25</time><div><b>Break</b>Stand up, drink water, no screens.</div></li><li><time>4:30</time><div><b>Block 2: Maths</b>Mark the questions. Redo the wrong ones.</div></li><li><time>5:00</time><div><b>Block 3: Biology</b>Blank-page summary of last week&rsquo;s topic.</div></li><li><time>5:30</time><div><b>Long break</b>Twenty minutes away from the desk.</div></li><li><time>5:50</time><div><b>Block 4: History</b>Flashcards from the &ldquo;missed&rdquo; pile.</div></li></ol></div><figure><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1719498861257-44589f1d6b73?auto=format&fit=crop&w=600&h=680&q=75" alt="people sitting at desks in a classroom" width="600" height="680" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Timed practice in a quiet room is the closest thing to the real exam.</figcaption></figure></div></section>

<section class="ex-s ex-tint"><div class="ex-w ex-fq"><p class="ex-eyebrow">Questions</p><h2>What students ask us</h2><div class="ex-faq"><details open><summary>How many hours a day should I revise?</summary><p>Quality matters more than the total. Three to five focused hours, in blocks of 25 to 50 minutes with breaks, is plenty for most students on a study day. Beyond that, attention drops and little more goes in.</p></details><details><summary>When should I start revising for an exam?</summary><p>Six to eight weeks before a major exam gives you time to cover the syllabus once, revisit the weak areas and finish with past papers. For a smaller test, two to three weeks is usually enough.</p></details><details><summary>What is the most effective revision technique?</summary><p>Active recall: closing your notes and trying to retrieve the material from memory, through flashcards, blank-page summaries or practice questions. It is harder than rereading and far more effective.</p></details><details><summary>How many past papers should I do?</summary><p>As many as you can mark and review properly. Three papers reviewed carefully, with every lost mark understood, teach more than ten papers completed and filed away.</p></details><details><summary>Is it better to revise one subject a day or mix them?</summary><p>Mix them. Two or three subjects a day, in separate blocks, keeps you fresher and improves long-term memory more than spending a whole day on a single subject.</p></details><details><summary>What should I do the night before an exam?</summary><p>Look over a one-page summary, pack your bag, check the time and place, and stop work early in the evening. A normal night of sleep helps more than three extra hours of cramming.</p></details></div></div></section>

<section class="ex-cta"><div class="ex-w"><h2>Not sure where to begin?</h2><p>Start with the timetable builder. It takes under a minute and tells you exactly how many sessions each subject needs.</p><a class="btn" href="#planners">Build my timetable</a></div></section>

